prose('REPORT.md', '''# Harappa edition and institutional access check

We recovered no new face bridge for the seven inherited cases. The missing-face queue remains 42. The useful result is a verified access distinction: Harappa exposes a 17-page scan through its article page, while our held chapter has 15 pages. The scan begins with the volume title and contents, followed by the chapter from printed xliv to lviii. Its bibliography ends with the same submitted-report list present on the held excerpt's final page. We checked these structural and ending correspondences; direct download returned 403, so we have not established byte identity or complete textual equivalence. We count no new chapter-content gain.

The [Harappa article page](https://www.harappa.com/content/inscribed-objects-harappa-excavations-1987-2007) links the [17-page file](https://www.harappa.com/sites/default/files/pdf/Kenoyer-Meadow-2010-HARP.pdf). The legacy URL redirects to old.harappa.com and was inaccessible to the web reader; direct acquisition of the legacy and linked files returned 403. The web reader supplied the title, contents and chapter text. Its contents page places HARP photographs at printed 207–360 and Harappa basic data at 423–441. Those catalogue sections are outside the 17-page file. They offer a precise future acquisition target without adding photographs to this run.

The primary bibliography, at printed lviii, identifies three annual reports by Meadow, Kenoyer and Wright: the 1998 report submitted 30 March 1999, the 1999 report submitted 30 July 2000, and the 2000–2001 report submitted December 2001. These publication leads fit the excavation years in the five HARP references. No inspected caption assigns any of their five attached figure numbers to one of these reports. The bibliography already occurs in the held excerpt, so this is citation provenance recovered from an available source, with no new primary object observation.

The [Oulu institutional record](https://www.finna.fi/Record/oy.9910978483906252) describes CISI 3.1 as a hardcover. The [Helka record](https://kansalliskirjasto.finna.fi/kansalliskirjastofikka/Record/helka.9934146360306253) describes CISI 3.2 as a 2019 hardcover and records an erroneous 2010 title-page year. Its exposed full-text link reaches an SFX resolver that reports no services for the record. Neither institutional route supplied plates. These findings apply to the inspected public records and resolver only.

Dholavira 341 and 348 retain their previous dispositions. The 348-to-20781 composite candidate still has an unresolved identifier namespace and an eight-versus-nine count disagreement. Object-identity-pass3 explains that the application accesses photographs through cisi and reports 284 anonymous filenames; that software and asset provenance supplies no independent object bridge. We inspected no new Dholavira body or image and admitted no physical merge.

The protocol was hashed before new source inspection. We used four route queries and six distinct public source or resolver routes, within the limits. Raw web responses, acquisition failures and the held text extract remain on D:. The first search response was not saved in full; its leads were followed through saved primary-page responses. New evidence is bounded to those preserved bodies. No image pixels were displayed; text exposure includes incidental catalogue references throughout the returned chapter text. Author checks validate the protocol, storage, hashes and seven-case accounting. Independent specialist review remains due. Bench is inapplicable to this source-access work.

The boldest supported reading is that the five HARP references are recoverable through the excavation reports or the catalogue's basic-data section, while the apparent two-page chapter extension supplies front matter. A caption matching a complete year, lot and item string to its figure, or the corresponding catalogue data row and photograph, would decide a face bridge.''')
